{"name":"Queen of the Night","hex":"#295776","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"queen of the night","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any night-blooming cereus, especially of species Epiphyllum oxypetalum or Selenicereus grandiflorus.","Night-blooming cestrum, a woody evergreen of species Cestrum nocturnum."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/queen_of_the_night"}],"entries":[{"title":"Night-blooming cereus","blocks":[{"text":"Night-blooming cereus is the common name referring to many flowering ceroid cacti that bloom at night. The flowers are short lived, and in some of these species, such as Selenicereus grandiflorus, each flower blooms for a single night, though most put out multiple flowers over several weeks, each of which opens for only a single night. Other names for one or more cacti with this habit are nightblooming frivolous, princess of the night, Honolulu queen, Christ in the manger, dama de noche, and queen of the night."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Nightbloomingcactusflorida.jpg/330px-Nightbloomingcactusflorida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night-blooming_cereus"},{"title":"Epiphyllum oxypetalum","blocks":[{"text":"Epiphyllum oxypetalum, the Dutchman's pipe cactus, princess of the night or queen of the night, is a species of cactus with a native range from Mexico to Nicaragua. It blooms nocturnally, and its flowers wilt before dawn. Though it is sometimes referred to as a night-blooming cereus, it is not closely related to any of the species in the tribe Cereeae that are more commonly known by that name. All Cereus species bloom at night and are terrestrial plants; Epiphyllum species are usually epiphytic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Epiphyllum_oxypetalum_flower.JPG/330px-Epiphyllum_oxypetalum_flower.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epiphyllum_oxypetalum"},{"title":"Queen of the Night (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Queen of the Night\" is a song co-written by American singer and actress Whitney Houston along with L.A. Reid, Babyface and Daryl Simmons. Produced by Reid and Babyface along with Simmons and Houston and performed by Houston, it was released in October 1993 by Arista Records as the fifth and final single from the soundtrack album The Bodyguard (1992), and is played during the closing credits of the film of the same name."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/50/Whitney_Houston_-_Queen_of_the_Night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_of_the_Night_(song)"},{"title":"Night","blocks":[{"text":"Night, or nighttime, is the period of darkness when the Sun is below the horizon. Daylight illuminates one side of Earth, leaving the other in darkness. The opposite of nighttime is daytime. Earth's rotation causes the appearance of sunrise and sunset. Moonlight, airglow, starlight, and light pollution dimly illuminate night. The duration of day, night, and twilight varies depending on the time of year and the observer's latitude."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg/330px-Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Selenicereus_grandiflorus00.jpg/330px-Selenicereus_grandiflorus00.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Selenicereus_grandiflorus00.jpg/330px-Selenicereus_grandiflorus00.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Selenicereus grandiflorus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASelenicereus_grandiflorus00.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Night-blooming_cereus-full-bloom.jpg/500px-Night-blooming_cereus-full-bloom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Night-blooming_cereus-full-bloom.jpg/1280px-Night-blooming_cereus-full-bloom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Night-blooming_cereus-full-bloom.jpg/500px-Night-blooming_cereus-full-bloom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Night-blooming cereus in full bloom in late August","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANight-blooming_cereus-full-bloom.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Epiphyllum_oxypetalum_%28Dutchman%27s_Pipe%2C_Night_Queen_or_%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%B6%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%97%E0%A4%82%E0%A4%A7%E0%A5%80_Nishagandhi_or_Gul-e-Bakawali%29.jpg/120px-Epiphyllum_oxypetalum_%28Dutchman%27s_Pipe%2C_Night_Queen_or_%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%B6%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%97%E0%A4%82%E0%A4%A7%E0%A5%80_Nishagandhi_or_Gul-e-Bakawali%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Epiphyllum_oxypetalum_%28Dutchman%27s_Pipe%2C_Night_Queen_or_%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%B6%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%97%E0%A4%82%E0%A4%A7%E0%A5%80_Nishagandhi_or_Gul-e-Bakawali%29.jpg/250px-Epiphyllum_oxypetalum_%28Dutchman%27s_Pipe%2C_Night_Queen_or_%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%B6%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%97%E0%A4%82%E0%A4%A7%E0%A5%80_Nishagandhi_or_Gul-e-Bakawali%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Epiphyllum_oxypetalum_%28Dutchman%27s_Pipe%2C_Night_Queen_or_%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A